    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d579">

                <pb facs="0779" n="767" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg767"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 EW/1–945: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Switzerland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Huddle</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-09T20:00:00-04:00">January 9, 1945—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>135. With reference to impending Swiss-German trade negotiations reported in
                    British Legation Bern’s 4174, December 29, Department believes Swiss should be
                    advised not to make any commitments which would preclude favorable agreement
                    with us for 1945 trade matters. You are therefore requested, at your discretion,
                    informally to indicate to the Swiss that any important commitments to Germans
                    would create unfavorable atmosphere for future negotiations with us and render
                    conclusion of satisfactory agreement most difficult, particularly as regards
                    Swiss desiderata. Perhaps the Swiss might arrange for an “escape clause”<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d579-f1"/> such as was included in the Swiss-German agreement
                    in September.</p>
                <p>It is hoped that your British colleague will join you in this informal approach
                    to the Swiss. If arrangements have been or are made shortly <pb facs="0780"
                        n="768" xml:id="pg768"/> for resumption of French participation on Mixed
                    Commission (Department’s 105, January 6,<anchor n="6" xml:id="d579-f2"/>
                    repeated to London as 138 and Paris as 63), we believe it would be helpful if
                    French also associated themselves with the approach.</p>
                <p>Sent to Bern repeated to Paris as 95 and to London as 193.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d579-f1" n="5" xml:id="d579-f1-content">
                <p>Term relates to provision in some trade agreements for a party to agreement to
                    withdraw certain concessions when unforeseen developments occur that threaten
                    serious injury to the producers of certain articles covered by the
                    agreement.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d579-f2" n="6" xml:id="d579-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
